<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Meluhha</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Meluḫḫa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Meluhha was a land known to ancient Mesopotamians, generally identified with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley civilisation</a>. It was reached by sea through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and supplied goods such as carnelian, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and other luxuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a>'s control of the Gulf trade gave it access to Meluhha's products.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meluhha.jpg" alt="Meluhha" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur; derivative work by Zunkir · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Moyen_Orient_3mil_aC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Meluhha (Sumerian Me-luh-ha) was the name Sumerian texts gave to a prominent trading partner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-bronze-age">Middle Bronze Age</a>. Its identification is an open question, but most scholars associate it with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation</a>. Sumerian texts repeatedly name three trading centres: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a>, now accepted as the area of the United Arab Emirates and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oman">Oman</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a>, taken to cover <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/failaka-island">Failaka Island</a> and the adjacent coast of Eastern Arabia, and Meluhha. Early texts such as the inscription of the Akkadian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rimush">Rimush</a> suggest it lay to the east. A much later text, the Rassam cylinder of the Assyrian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, seems to place it in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sargon-of-akkad">Sargon of Akkad</a> referred to ships coming from Meluhha, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a>, and his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naram-sin">Naram-Sin</a> named a man of Meluhha among rebel kings. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lagash">Lagash</a> recorded that people of Meluhha came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> to sell gold dust and carnelian, and that he obtained blocks of lapis lazuli and carnelian from Meluhha. Meluhha is named in the myth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enki">Enki</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ninhursag">Ninhursag</a> as a land that would load carnelian and fine woods into large ships. A king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibbi-sin">Ibbi-Sin</a>, received a speckled or red Meluhha dog as tribute, perhaps a dhole.</p><p>A settlement of Meluhhans stood in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumer">Sumer</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girsu">Girsu</a>, probably at Gu'abba, and most references to it date from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-dynasty-of-ur">Ur III</a> period. Direct trade seems to have faded in the Ur III period and to have been replaced by trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dilmun">Dilmun</a>, possibly in step with the end of the cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus-valley">Indus Valley</a>. Rimush claimed to have defeated Meluhha troops in the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elam">Elam</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gudea">Gudea</a> claimed victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magan">Magan</a>, Meluhha, Elam and Amurru.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Several <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indus">Indus</a> seals have been found in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ur">Ur</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kish">Kish</a>, and a carnelian monkey figurine from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/susa">Susa</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a> may have come from the Indus region. The archaeological record of the trade is thin, however: Andrew Robinson counts about twenty Indus seals in Mesopotamia, nine dated to the Akkadian period, and a single Indus weight from Ur. Trade goods included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebony">ebony</a> and lapis lazuli, mined at the Harappan colony of Shortugai in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. Asko Parpola derives the name from Dravidian words for a high place and links it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> word mleccha, meaning foreigner.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Meluhha” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…the head of the Persian Gulf and so controlled the maritime route connecting Mesopotamia to Dilmun (Bahrain), Magan (Oman) and <b>Meluhha</b> (the Indus Valley), which brought copper, stone, timber and luxury goods to resource-poor Mesopotamia; Sargon declared that ships from these lands moored at the quay of Akkad.</blockquote><a href="/empire/akkadian-empire">Akkadian Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Sargon of Akkad later claimed that ships from Dilmun, Magan and <b>Meluhha</b> moored at the quay of his capital Agade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dilmun">Dilmun · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Mesopotamian texts speak of <b>Meluhha</b>, a distant land reached by ship, and most scholars identify it with the Indus civilisation.</blockquote><a href="/culture/indus-valley-civilisation">Indus Valley Civilisation · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>Magan appears in cuneiform texts from the 23rd century BCE, when Sargon of Akkad recorded that ships from Magan, <b>Meluhha</b> and Dilmun moored at his capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/magan">Magan</a></li></ul></section>
